          </mxGeometry>
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="e5" value="saves" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;rounded=0;html=1;exitX=0;exitY=0.6613;exitDx=0;exitDy=0;entryX=1;entryY=0.7941;entryDx=0;entryDy=0;strokeColor=#000000;strokeWidth=2;fontColor=#000000;fontFamily=Helvetica;fontSize=12;labelBackgroundColor=#FFFFFF;startArrow=ERzeroToMany;startFill=0;startSize=14;endArrow=ERmandOne;endFill=0;endSize=14;jumpStyle=arc;jumpSize=10;dashed=0;" edge="1" parent="1" source="t0" target="t3">
          <mxGeometry x="-0.82" relative="1" as="geometry">
            <Array as="points">
              <mxPoint x="445" y="142"/>
              <mxPoint x="445" y="142"/>
            </Array>
          </mxGeometry>
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="n0" value="&lt;b&gt;Resolving many&amp;#8209;to&amp;#8209;many&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;A pet has many traits and a trait describes many pets. No relational table can hold that directly, so each one becomes a junction table whose primary key is the pair of foreign keys &amp;#8212; marked &lt;b&gt;PF&lt;/b&gt;. The pair being the key is what makes the same pet and trait impossible to record twice.&lt;br&gt;The three below are the only composite keys in the schema." style="text;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fontSize=15;fontFamily=Helvetica;fontColor=#000000;strokeColor=#000000;fillColor=#FFFFFF;strokeWidth=2;spacing=16;whiteSpace=wrap;overflow=width;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="60" y="636" width="2140" height="88" as="geometry"/>
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="n1" value='&lt;b&gt;1NF &amp;#8212; no repeating groups, no lists in a column&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;The tempting shortcut is a &lt;i&gt;traits&lt;/i&gt; column on &lt;b&gt;pets&lt;/b&gt; holding "calm, good&amp;#8209;with&amp;#8209;kids, house&amp;#8209;trained". It breaks the first thing anyone will ask the database: &lt;i&gt;show me every calm dog&lt;/i&gt; becomes a LIKE over a string, it cannot be indexed, and nothing stops "calm" and "Calm" being two different traits.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Every repeating group here is its own table instead: &lt;b&gt;pet_traits&lt;/b&gt;, &lt;b&gt;option_traits&lt;/b&gt;, &lt;b&gt;favorites&lt;/b&gt;, &lt;b&gt;pet_photos&lt;/b&gt;, &lt;b&gt;boarding_instructions&lt;/b&gt;, &lt;b&gt;quiz_answers&lt;/b&gt;. Each cell in this schema holds one value.' style="text;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fontSize=15;fontFamily=Helvetica;fontColor=#000000;strokeColor=#000000;fillColor=#FFFFFF;strokeWidth=2;spacing=16;whiteSpace=wrap;overflow=width;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="60" y="770" width="1040" height="160" as="geometry"/>
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="n2" value='&lt;b&gt;2NF &amp;#8212; every non&amp;#8209;key column depends on the whole key&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Only the three junction tables have composite keys, and the way they pass 2NF is that they have &lt;b&gt;no non&amp;#8209;key columns at all&lt;/b&gt;: &lt;b&gt;pet_traits&lt;/b&gt; is exactly (pet_id, trait_id), and &lt;b&gt;option_traits&lt;/b&gt; exactly (option_id, trait_id). There is nothing that could depend on half a key.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;The label and slug of a trait live once, on &lt;b&gt;traits&lt;/b&gt;. Renaming "good with kids" is one UPDATE of one row, not an update of every pet that has it &amp;#8212; which is the whole point of the normal form.' style="text;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fontSize=15;fontFamily=Helvetica;fontColor=#000000;strokeColor=#000000;fillColor=#FFFFFF;strokeWidth=2;spacing=16;whiteSpace=wrap;overflow=width;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="60" y="970" width="1040" height="160" as="geometry"/>
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="n3" value="&lt;b&gt;3NF &amp;#8212; no column depends on another non&amp;#8209;key column&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;A shelter's city belongs to &lt;b&gt;shelters&lt;/b&gt;, not repeated on every pet housed there; a vaccine's name belongs to &lt;b&gt;vaccine_types&lt;/b&gt;, not retyped on every dose; a question's text belongs to &lt;b&gt;quiz_questions&lt;/b&gt;, not copied into every answer. Each fact is written in exactly one place, and every other table reaches it by key." style="text;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fontSize=15;fontFamily=Helvetica;fontColor=#000000;strokeColor=#000000;fillColor=#FFFFFF;strokeWidth=2;spacing=16;whiteSpace=wrap;overflow=width;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="60" y="1170" width="1040" height="124" as="geometry"/>
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="n4" value='&lt;b&gt;Where this schema stores derived data on purpose&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Five places break the rule knowingly. Listing them is more useful than claiming they are not there:&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;adoption_applications.applicant_name / _email / _phone / _address / _city&lt;/b&gt; &amp;#8212; copied from &lt;b&gt;users&lt;/b&gt; at submission. Not redundancy: a &lt;i&gt;snapshot&lt;/i&gt;. The application must still say where the applicant lived when they applied, after they move.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;adoptions.shelter_id&lt;/b&gt; &amp;#8212; reachable through the pet today, but a pet can be returned and re&amp;#8209;listed elsewhere. The completed adoption has to keep naming the shelter that actually handed the animal over.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;quiz_attempts.score / max_score / passed&lt;/b&gt; &amp;#8212; recomputable from &lt;b&gt;quiz_answers&lt;/b&gt;, stored because the scoring weights on &lt;b&gt;quiz_options&lt;/b&gt; can be edited afterwards, and a past attempt must not silently change its result.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;pets.view_count&lt;/b&gt; &amp;#8212; a counter. Deriving it would mean keeping a row per page view, which is far more storage for a number nobody audits.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;pet_photos.cover_flag&lt;/b&gt; and &lt;b&gt;adoption_applications.pending_flag&lt;/b&gt; &amp;#8212; generated columns, never written by the application. They exist because MySQL has no partial unique index: the flag is NULL except on the row that must be unique, so UNIQUE(pet_id, cover_flag) enforces "at most one cover photo per pet" and UNIQUE(applicant_id, pet_id, pending_flag) enforces "at most one open application per person per pet". Both are constraints wearing the costume of a column.' style="text;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fontSize=15;fontFamily=Helvetica;fontColor=#000000;strokeColor=#000000;fillColor=#FFFFFF;strokeWidth=2;spacing=16;whiteSpace=wrap;overflow=width;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="1160" y="770" width="1040" height="376" as="geometry"/>
        </mxCell>
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
</mxfile>
